# A local revika DHT network: one seed node plus two peers that bootstrap
# through it. Because a node's Peer ID is derived from an identity key it
# generates on first start, bootstrapping is a two-step dance:
#
#   1. Start the seed and read its Peer ID from the logs:
#        docker compose up  seed    # copy the "Peer ID:" value
#
#   2. Put the seed's in-network multiaddr in .env, then start the peers:
#        echo 'SEED_ADDR=/dns4/seed/tcp/4001/p2p/<PeerID-from-step-1>' > .env
#        docker compose up -d node2 node3
#
# The seed's on-disk identity lives on the named volume, so its Peer ID (and
# thus SEED_ADDR) stays stable across restarts. Nodes reach each other by
# service name on the compose network; only the seed is published to the host.
#
# A client then uses the *host-published* seed address as its bootstrap peer:
#   go run ./cmd/revika-ctl put -bootstrap /ip4/127.0.0.1/tcp/4001/p2p/<PeerID> file.bin
SEED_ADDR=/dns4/seed/tcp/4001/p2p/<PeerID-from-step-1>
